preConditionHook:
// BUY THE FIRST BEAR-CLOSING BAR INSIDE A BULL SPIKE. After runBars consecutive bull
// trend bars (bars 1..runBars), the current bar closes DOWN: Brooks buys it at
// the next open, expecting the countertrend bar to fail immediately. Short is
// the mirror -- a bear run followed by an up close.
//
// NOT COMPOSABLE: the long side needs a BEAR bar at lag 0 and BULL bars at lags
// 1..runBars, and a placement's flip toggle reverses both halves at once. Nor
// can a pane supply the run at lag 1, since the run must END one bar before the
// signal bar.
//
// Distinct from SpikePullbackLimitFade, which takes the same run and enters on
// a bar that POKES below the prior bar's low with no requirement that it close
// down; the two fire on different bars.
//
// The run's bar shape is StrongTrendBarRun's settled definition read at lag 1:
// a strict body direction and a body of at least bodyFraction of the bar's own
// range. The tails are deliberately NOT tested -- this is the trend-bar run,
// not the strong-trend-bar run of ConsecutiveStrongTrendBars.
//
// The all-of-N loops start true and let any failing bar clear the flag, so the
// loops run unconditionally and EasyLanguage's lack of short-circuiting cannot
// matter. The flags are class members and persist across bars, so both are
// reset at the top.
//
// Products, not quotients: EasyLanguage raises a runtime divide-by-zero and a
// zero-range bar is legal data.
//
// Deepest bar touched: runBars.
runUp = true;
runDn = true;
for (int k = 1; k <= (int)runBars; ++k) {
    if (el_le(close[k], open[k])) runUp = false;
    if (el_lt(close[k] - open[k], bodyFraction * (high[k] - low[k]))) runUp = false;
    if (el_ge(close[k], open[k])) runDn = false;
    if (el_lt(open[k] - close[k], bodyFraction * (high[k] - low[k]))) runDn = false;
}
